    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Steel edging is often described as the "set it and forget it" solution for lawn and border edges. Its durability, slim profile and ability to hold crisp, clean lines make it a favourite with garden designers and homeowners alike. The "forget it" part, though, is a common misconception. Steel is far more resilient than plastic or timber, but it is still a metal exposed to shifting soil, changing temperatures, moisture and the odd knock from garden tools.</p>
<p>To keep your garden looking as sharp as the day the edging went in, a simple seasonal routine is worth the effort. It keeps the lines looking good and catches small problems before they turn into bigger repairs. This guide breaks down what to do for steel edging throughout the year.</p>

<h2 id="spring-inspection">1. Spring inspection</h2>
<p>Spring is the most important time for steel edging maintenance. As the ground thaws after winter, freezing and thawing soil (frost heave) can make even well-anchored sections shift, rise or tilt.</p>
<p>Start with a slow walk along the full length of the edging and check that it still sits at the intended height relative to the soil. If heaving has pushed it up, it can become a trip hazard and a risk to mower blades. Use a rubber mallet and a block of wood to tap it gently back down to its proper depth; the block spreads the blow and protects the top edge.</p>
<p>Inspect the stakes and joining clips too. Freezing ground can push stakes back up, so make sure every anchor is firmly seated in the subsoil. If a section has bowed outwards under the weight of wet spring soil, dig out a little soil behind it, reposition the strip and backfill with well-compacted soil or crushed stone.</p>
<p>Wear sturdy gloves whenever you handle the edging. Quality landscape steel usually has a rolled or capped top edge, but cut ends, joints and any spot that has been struck by a mower can be sharp.</p>

<h2 id="summer-grooming">2. Summer grooming</h2>
<p>In summer the main challenge is fast-growing grass and weeds. Steel edging is a good physical barrier to roots, but it cannot stop grass creeping over the top or debris collecting at the seam, where weeds then take hold.</p>
<p>Summer care is mostly about looks and protecting the finish. When you strimmer along the edge, be mindful of the coating. Weathering steel is designed to develop a rust-coloured surface, but powder-coated or galvanised steel can be chipped by heavy strimmer line. Once the coating is broken, the steel underneath can start to rust early, so keep the line light and angled away from the metal.</p>
<p>In full sun, dark metal gets hot. Plants right against the edging may dry out a little faster than the rest of the border, so keep an eye on them during dry spells. A fresh layer of mulch up to the edging helps keep the soil next to it cool and moist.</p>

<h2 id="autumn-preservation">3. Autumn preservation</h2>
<p>As the growing season winds down, shift your focus to clearing debris and managing moisture. Autumn leaves piled against steel edging hold water against the metal for weeks, which can speed up corrosion, especially at the soil line.</p>
<p>Rake leaves away from the edging regularly through autumn. This is also the time to check for undermining: heavy rain can wash soil away from one side, leaving a gap. If water pools against the steel or runs underneath it, it can erode the edge of your beds.</p>
<p>Fill any gaps with compacted topsoil or a heavy mulch so water does not find an easy channel that becomes a bigger erosion point by spring. In exposed, windy gardens, check that the edging is still firmly seated before the first frosts.</p>

<h2 id="winter-awareness">4. Winter awareness</h2>
<p>Winter maintenance is mostly passive, but it pays to stay alert. The biggest threat to steel edging in winter is not the cold; it is the snow shovel. If your edging borders a drive or path, it is easy to catch the metal with a shovel blade or snow clearer.</p>
<p>Before snow arrives, mark the run of the edging with garden canes or reflective snow markers wherever you will be clearing snow. This helps prevent knocks that bend the steel or break the joints.</p>
<p>De-icing salt is the other winter concern. Avoid rock salt near metal edging, as salt can attack galvanised and powder-coated finishes. If you need a de-icer on nearby paths, check the product label for its suitability near metal and plants. If salt spray from the road builds up on the edging, rinse it off with plain water during the first mild spell of late winter.</p>

<h2 id="corrosion-control">5. Corrosion control</h2>
<p>It helps to understand the difference between "good" and "bad" rust. Weathering steel is designed to form a stable, rust-coloured surface layer (a patina) that slows further corrosion. Maintenance here means making sure the patina can form evenly: if one area sits in water constantly, it never dries properly and may flake rather than settle. Be aware that run-off from weathering steel, particularly while the patina is forming, can stain nearby paving, gravel and light-coloured stone, so check the manufacturer's guidance on where it is suitable.</p>
<p>On powder-coated or galvanised steel, rust is a warning sign. If you spot a scratch or chip where rust is starting, deal with it promptly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Remove loose rust with a wire brush or fine sandpaper.</li>
<li>Clean the area with a suitable cleaner and let it dry.</li>
<li>Apply an outdoor metal primer and then a matching touch-up paint, following the product labels.</li>
<li>If you use any solvent-based products, wear gloves and make sure you have good ventilation.</li>
</ul>
<p>This small job stops rust creeping under the rest of the coating. How long any steel edging lasts depends on the product, the thickness of the steel, your soil and climate, and how well it is looked after; check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>

<h2 id="soil-compaction">6. Soil compaction</h2>
<p>The stability of steel edging depends on the firmness of the soil around it. Over time, especially after heavy rain or a long dry spell, soil can shrink away from the metal and leave the edging free to wobble.</p>
<p>Once a year, typically in spring or autumn, do a compaction check. Walk the length of the edging and firm the soil on both sides with your heel or a hand tamper, so the pressure from the lawn side is balanced by the border side. If the soil has settled a lot, top it up with a firm, loamy soil and compact it to give the strip a solid socket to sit in. Most of the strip should sit below ground, as the manufacturer specifies.</p>

<h2 id="mechanical-protection">7. Mechanical protection</h2>
<p>Maintenance also means protecting your other equipment from the steel. Edging is thin and hard, and a mower blade that strikes it can be damaged, as can the edging itself.</p>
<p>Set the mower cutting height so the blades clear the edging comfortably, and check this clearance each season, because edging that has heaved can rise into the blade's path. If it has risen, reset it before you mow. With a powered lawn edger (the type with a vertical spinning blade), keep the edger blade a little away from the steel; metal-on-metal contact blunts the blade and damages the edging's finish.</p>
<p>After any strike, run a gloved hand carefully along the top edge and check for sharp burrs. File off any you find, then touch up the coating as described above.</p>

<h2 id="alignment">8. Alignment</h2>
<p>Once a year, stand back and look at the lines of your garden. Small ground movements can gradually turn straight runs wavy and smooth curves uneven.</p>
<p>Steel edging can usually be adjusted even after years in the ground. If a line looks off, set out a string line to find the true path. Use a spade to make a small relief cut in the soil on the side you want to move the edging towards, then, wearing gloves, use a pry bar to lever the metal gently back into line, working a little at a time rather than forcing one spot. Firm the soil on both sides afterwards to lock it in its corrected position.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep a small kit by the shed door for edging checks: gloves, a rubber mallet, a wood block, a hand tamper and a touch-up pot for your edging's finish. Having it to hand makes the seasonal walk-round a quick job.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does all steel edging eventually rust through?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Plain carbon steel will oxidise over time, but how quickly depends on the thickness of the steel, the finish and the conditions. Weathering steel is designed to form a protective rust-coloured layer, and galvanised or powder-coated steel holds up well if the coating is maintained. Lifespan varies with product, soil, climate and care, so check the manufacturer's warranty.</p>
<h3>Can I use steel edging in coastal areas with salt air?</h3>
<p>Yes, but it needs more attention. Salt air speeds up corrosion, so stainless steel or well-coated galvanised steel is a better choice than raw steel near the coast. Rinsing the edging with fresh water from time to time also helps.</p>
<h3>How deep should steel edging be buried?</h3>
<p>Most of the strip should sit below ground, as the manufacturer specifies for your product. This gives it the grip it needs to resist the weight of the soil and stops it leaning over time.</p>
<h3>What tools do I need for seasonal maintenance?</h3>
<p>The basics are gloves, a rubber mallet, a wood block to protect the metal when tapping it, a spade or trowel for soil adjustments, a hand tamper (or firm boots) for compaction, and a wire brush and touch-up paint for the finish.</p>
<h3>Is steel edging dangerous for pets or children?</h3>
<p>Landscape-grade steel edging usually has a rolled or capped top edge to reduce the risk of cuts. During your seasonal checks, look for sharp burrs left by mower strikes, edging that has heaved up into a trip hazard, and stakes that have lifted out of the ground.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Well-kept steel edging is one of the simplest ways to make a garden look considered. A few hours each season spent inspecting, clearing, compacting and touching up will keep the lines crisp from spring growth through to winter. If you are still choosing a material, compare the options in <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a>, follow our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-build-landscape-edging">how to build landscape edging</a>, or see <a href="/blog/stone-edging-maintenance-by-season">stone edging maintenance by season</a> if your borders mix materials. For help designing a garden or terrace where every edge, path and border works together, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
